he circuit topology (MPC/FHE). This closes the explicit I/O channel — the only deliberate way out is the return value. In the controlled mode that means a capability authorizing no effects: handlers present but refused (an empty allow-set is equivalent to stripping), not absent by construction.\ndocs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md:370:host cannot safely evaluate remain explicit refusals. Cross-host\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:932:  scheduler as `semantic.cljc:885`; `eval` refuses an AST as `:865-876`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.typecheck.md:42:   an evaluator. A refused program never lands on an evaluator's medium, so\ntest/dao/space/query_aggregates_test.cljc:30:   - Numeric carriers a host cannot evaluate are explicit refusals, not\ntest/dao/space/query_aggregates_test.cljc:239:;; Numeric carriers: the host that cannot evaluate them refuses explicitly\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md:257:The two delivery states of §1.1 are the states at which the evaluator parks by itself. They are special cases of its general state between any two instructions, `⟨A, pc, W↾L(pc), E, K⟩`, which `vm-hot` materializes whenever fuel runs out (`semantic.cljc:142-143`) and which the carrier expresses as the `run` state of §1.2. A step is atomic: effects are observed only inside a step or inside the scheduler's sweep, so no state between steps holds a half-issued effect; atomicity of a step is not transactionality of the outside world (an append performed before a host exception is not undone). A task is a **product**: at most one active activation (the `run`), its ordered pending waits, its ordered completed wakes (the ready queue: a woken wait with the value, status and cursor update its effect produced, carried by the `:ready` row of §2.3), its parked table, and its stores. The scheduler constructs every combination: resuming one of several woken waits leaves the others queued and the unresolved ones pending (`engine.cljc:2294-2312`, `:2305`). Every such state is **representable**. The lift refuses only what is not **portable** (a value with no wire form), not **satisfied** (code or dependencies the receiver lacks), or a **hold** (a driver-internal observation in flight: §7.1 step 1), never a pc. The static correspondence of §4 holds at every pc of a body: distinct virtuals in `L(p) ∪ def(p)` hold distinct physical registers (`derive/allocation`), and the stack kernel's operand region at A pc `p` is `stack-layout(p) ++ uses(p)` above a dead prefix (§4.3); both are checks (§5.6), not theorems. A root whose task has an active activation has kind `:running` (§2.6) and is a custody subject like a blocked or parked root (§7.3).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.v2.divergence-register.md:330:1. **`eval` refuses an AST.** The walker's `vm/eval` converts, loads, and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:1640:`IVM`/`IVMState` behavior matches (including eval/environment refusals),\ntest/yin/repl/index_test.cljc:780:      (is (false? (:ingress-loss? lost)) \"the evaluator is not refused\")\ntest/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc:333:                           (app (app (v 'code/entry) (lit 'foo) (lit address) (lit 'f))))\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/depth_baseline_fbd8da0f.edn:2:{:revision \"fbd8da0f\", :workload-count 155, :overlap-count 155, :workloads {:minimal-last {:walker 1, :semantic 5}, :parse-bad-binding {:walker 1, :semantic 9}, :beta-eq-false {:walker 1, :semantic 9}, :syntax-term-refusal {:walker 1, :semantic 7}, :opacity-context-refusal {:walker 1, :semantic 13}, :rebuild-type-explicit-supply {:walker 1, :semantic 14}, :h3-3-beta-reduction-nonredex {:walker 1, :semantic 7}, :h3-3-unregister-unparser-absent {:walker 1, :semantic 5}, :lookup-safe-get {:walker 1, :semantic 5}, :parse-reserved {:walker 1, :semantic 6}, :display-deftype-theorem {:walker 1, :semantic 6}, :type-of-invalid {:walker 1, :semantic 8}, :h3-2-have-duplicate {:walker 1, :semantic 18}, :h3-3-parse-one {:walker 1, :semantic 10}, :h3-3-binderify {:walker 1, :seman